post.description:
Calls the specified tool with the provided arguments and returns the result in MCP format.

**Example - List Sequences:**

```json
{
"name": "platform_list_capabilities",
"arguments": {"category": "Sequences"}
}
```

**Example - Get Action Details:**

```json
{
"name": "platform_get_action_details",
"arguments": {"operationId": "get_sequences.list_sequences"}
}
```

**Example - Execute Action:**

```json
{
"name": "platform_execute_action",
"arguments": {
"operationId": "get_agents.list_agents"
}
}
```

The response follows MCP format with a `content` array containing the result.

get.description:
Returns server capabilities, protocol version, and metadata.

**Connecting to this MCP Server:**

**Claude Desktop** - Add to `claude_desktop_config.json`:

```json
{
"mcpServers": {
"platform": {
"command": "npx",
"args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", "https://api.example.com/api/v0/mcp"],
"env": {"API_KEY": "your_api_key"}
}
}
}
```

**Generic MCP Client:**

```bash
npx mcp-remote https://api.example.com/api/v0/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY"
```

**Protocol Version:** 2024-11-05

schema.description:
Bulk-update rows in one request, like SQL UPDATE <list> SET <set> WHERE <where>. `set` is the column→value assignment applied to every matching row; `where` is a conditions tree (the SAME shape used for action run-conditions) selecting which rows to update. Updating EVERY row requires passing `matchAll: true` explicitly (and no `where`) — a missing or condition-less `where` alone is a 400, never a match-all. A `where` that is present but is not a conditions tree is also a 400, and so is any body key other than set/where/limit/matchAll/companyId (e.g. a `rows` array — that is not how rows are targeted here).

Example — set Status and Tier on all tech-industry rows:
{
  "set": {"Status": "qualified", "Tier": "A"},
  "where": {
    "combinator": "AND",
    "filters": [{"field": "Company Industry", "operator": "contains", "value": "Tech"}],
    "groups": []
  }
}

Example — update exactly ONE row by its id. Use the virtual `rowId` field inside a normal filter; do NOT pass a bare {"rowId": "..."} shorthand as `where`:
{
  "set": {"Status": "CANNOT SUBMIT"},
  "where": {
    "combinator": "AND",
    "filters": [{"field": "rowId", "operator": "equals", "value": "6c9a36cc-0dae-41fd-9ccb-c5688c46bc7c"}],
    "groups": []
  }
}
